Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
> 
> On Wed, 1 Mar 2006, Andy Johnson wrote:
>> I can imagine that if monster rings were implemented there would be a 
>> fairly small set of rings the monsters would wear:
>>
>> [foo] Resistance
>> Conflict
> 
>   Personally, I'm not a fan of the =oConflict idea that started this 
> thread. Stormbringer-like effects are too much like gratuitous 
> instadeath in some situations, and I think that's the /best/ of the 
> options suggested so far. Face it: conflict does things to monsters that 
> can't be simulated on the player character.

I agree that conflict couldn't work the same way on a PC, however I 
think it's a good candidate to cause a local change to gameplay 
mechanics, perhaps acting as if the player had another intrinsic similar 
to Aggrivate Monster. Presumably the ring couldn't have a beneficial 
effect for the player or the monster just wouldn't wear it. I think from 
most monsters' point of view, standard conflict is bad (although the 
player might disagree if they have a pet Archon, the enemies don't think 
all that hard).

>> Invisibility
>> Polymorph
> 
>   Eh?  I know they can jump onto poly traps, but =oPoly take a long time 
> to kick in, don't they?  This wouldn't be on my short list.

This one would essentially be turning enemies into chameleons. It 
doesn't necessarily have to have the same rate of polymorph for monsters 
as for players. Chameleons shift often enough that the battle can get 
interesting.

>> Regeneration
>> Teleportation
> 
>   See =oPoly.

Admittedly, Teleportitis is not generally considered a good thing. This 
ring would give monsters the effects of a Tengu; could be pretty 
annoying if you're trying to track down a specific foe.

> 
> Rings I'd add that you didn't: invisibility, see invisible, protection, 
> increase accuracy, increase damage.

Invisibility's on my list. See invisible might be useful but I would not 
rate it as a high priority because player Invisibility has a very small 
effect on gameplay. The last three only change the monster stats a bit, 
and again don't really affect gameplay (unless it was like a +50 ring of 
damage).

I was going for the rings that could spice up an encounter and hopefully 
add more depth to combat.

> 
>   I just hacked together a patch that ought to let monsters pick up and 
> use some of those rings; I'm going to test it now and see what happens.
> If L beats me in the race to create this patch, oh well. ;-)

Excellent work! I look forward to trying it out.


